1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 {First, I want to show several very poor ways for Black to play and explain the thought process of a strong chess player.} Nf6 {The Petroff Defence. For now, there is nothing wrong with this move.} (2...
Bc5 $2 {The move leaves e5 en prise... This is the first thing to check, and if there is no clear refutation, you play it.} 3. Nxe5 Qh4 {Black attacks f2 and e4. But White is already a pawn up, so returning it is not a problem.} (3... Qf6 {[%cal Rf6f2,Rf6e5,Yc5f2] leads to the same thing.}
4. d4 $18) 4. d4 {Golden opening rules: the centre.} Qxe4+ 5. Be3 {Defends d4.}
Bb6 6. Nc3 $16 {In the end, White has a huge lead in development and therefore an advantage.}) 3. Nxe5 Nc6 $6 {A bad gambit that nevertheless enjoys a certain popularity online.} (3... Nxe4 $2 4. Qe2 {Causes problems because of a discovered check.} Nf6 5. Nc6+ $18) (3... d6 4. Nf3 Nxe4 {But here Black has fully adequate play.}) 4. Nxc6 dxc6 {Black's biggest problem is not only White's extra pawn, but above all White's strong centre. White must now follow the golden opening rules.} 5. d3 {Since White cannot achieve d4, this is best. White retains complete flexibility and the pawn is safely defended.} (5. Nc3 {This is not a mistake in itself. However, Nc3 only makes sense if White intends to achieve d4. After ...Bc5 this will not be possible, so d3 is more precise because it leaves more options.} Bc5 6. d3 $2 (6. Be2
{Prevents ...Ng4; its only drawback is that it returns the pawn.} Qd4
7. O-O Nxe4 $11 {The position is equal, but White is not losing.}) (6. h3
$1 {This move is tactically justified because it holds up concretely, but I would identify 5.Nc3 as the "mistake".} Qd4 7. Qf3) (6. Bc4 $2 {Develops the bishop to its best square, but this is very risky because Black has the initiative. It would be safer to remain passive until the king is secure; concretely, White also has a problem.} Bxf2+ {This is enough to "refute" Bc4.} (6... Ng4 {Is even better.} 7. O-O Qh4 $19) 7. Kxf2 Qd4+) 6... Ng4
$17) (5. e5 {Should not be the first thought, although it is not a bad move. White has to weaken the centre slightly.} Nd5 6. d4 $1 {But at least White achieves d4 and keeps the pawn.}) 5... Bc5 6. Be3 $5 {When you are a pawn up, you should exchange pieces. This already refutes the gambit, even if objectively it is not the strongest move of all.} (6. Be2 {Is technically best, after which Black has problems.} h5 {Black's last trick.} (6... O-O 7. O-O $16 {[%cal Gc2c3,Yc1e3]}) 7. c3 $1 {[%cal Gd3d4] The centre always comes first.} Be6 8. d4 Bb6 9. Qc2 Qd7 10. Bg5 O-O-O 11. Nd2 {[%cal Ye1g1,Ye1c1] White has followed the opening rules and has a winning position. White can also castle queenside.}) 6... Bxe3 7. fxe3 $14 {You do not always have to play the absolute best move. Sometimes the simplest good solution is enough.} *

