{The following game fragment comes from a game between players rated approximately 1700 and 1400.} 1. c4 e6 2. g3 d5 3. cxd5 $6 {White exchanges the c4-pawn for the e6-pawn. This is particularly unfavourable here: Bc8 can develop, while Bg2 stares into empty space.} (3. Bg2 {Is better. The pawn is not hanging.} dxc4 4. Qa4+
{This line looks strange because it does not arise from a classical opening (1.d4 or 1.e4), so more complex strategies are required. This is also why I would not recommend that beginners immediately play such sidelines merely to trick their opponents.}) 3... exd5 4. Bg2 Nf6 5. d4 Nc6 $2 {In d4 openings, one should not normally play ...Nc6 and block the c-pawn. Concretely, ...Nc6 does nothing here either. d4 is well defended, b4 does not need defending, and the knight has no future.} (5... b6 {[%cal Gc7c5] This would be a bad plan, but still better than ...Nc6.}) (5... c5 {Would normally be standard, but here it would leave Black with an isolated pawn.}) (5... c6 6. Nf3 Bf5
7. Bf4 Nbd7 8. Nc3 Be7 9. O-O O-O $15 {This would be a more harmonious setup and the best continuation. ...Bf5 is active, and Bg2 points towards d5.}) *

