{I played Black in this online game.} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3.
Bb5 Nf6 4. Nc3 Bb4 5. Bxc6 dxc6 6. Nxe5 $6 {White should know that this does not win a pawn. In that case, Bxc6 followed by Nxe5 makes no sense at all.} Bxc3
7. bxc3 (7. dxc3 Qxd1+ 8. Kxd1 Nxe4 {Is slightly better for Black.}) 7... Qe7
$5 (7... Nxe4 8. Qe2 {Black considered this unpleasant.}) 8. d4 Nxe4 {[%cal Re4c3] White must now make an unpleasant decision. How should White defend c3? What do the opening rules say?} 9. Qd3 (9. c4 {The centre, but there is an unpleasant reply.} Qb4+) (9. O-O $3 {The opening rules say nothing about defending pawns, and that is in fact the best move here!} Nxc3 $140 10. Qf3 {[%cal Rc1a3,Rc2c4,Rf1e1] White gets a powerful attack for the pawn.} Nd5 11. Re1 O-O 12. Ba3 {With a material advantage.}) 9... Bf5 10. Qe3 $2 {Moving the same piece twice in the opening is wrong. The punishment comes immediately.} (10. O-O {There is no dangerous discovered attack here.} Nxf2 (10... Ng3 11. Qxg3) 11. Qxf5) 10... f6 11. Nc4 Nxc3 {Black is a pawn up.} 12. Qxe7+ Kxe7 13. Bb2 Nd5 14. O-O $2 {And White also loses the next pawn.} Bxc2 {Now Black only needs to exchange pieces and reach the endgame.} 15. Rfe1+ Kd7 16. Rac1 Bg6 17. Ne3 Rhe8 {Exchange of pieces.} 18. Nxd5 Rxe1+ 19. Rxe1 cxd5 20. Ba3 Re8 {Exchange of pieces.} 21. Rb1
$2 Bxb1 *

