{The following example comes from a classical cup match: a player rated about 1400 faced me when I was rated about 2300.} 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. g3 dxc4
5. Bg2 c6 {Black is saying that the c4-pawn must be kept at all costs. This is risky, although it can be done with caution; if the price becomes too high, Black should be prepared to return the pawn.} 6. a4 {The sole purpose of this move is to prevent b5.} Bb4+ (6... b5 $6 7. Ne5 {[%cal Rg2a8,Ga4b5]} Bb7 $4 8. axb5 cxb5 9. Bxb7) 7. Bd2 Qa5 $2 {This move violates the opening rules...} (7... c5 {How do you find this move? If you know the queenside setup, it is easy. The fact that Black has lost a tempo does not matter because White also played a4.}) (7... a5 {This would be "best", but Black would have to return the pawn.} 8. Qc2 O-O
9. Qxc4) 8. O-O b5 {Black plays only for the pawn, but a pawn becomes relevant only in the endgame.} 9. Ne5 Bb7 10. Bxb4 Qxb4 11. axb5 {Since Black cannot play ...cxb5, the extra c4-pawn is already lost.} Qxb2 {Wins an unimportant pawn and gives White even more time.} 12. Na3 {[%cal Ya3c4]} Nd5 13.
Naxc4 Qb4 (13... Qxb5 $2 14. Nd6+) 14. Rb1 Qe7 15. bxc6 {The sad outcome is that Black did not even keep the pawn, but lost so much time that the position collapses tactically. If only Black had still been able to castle...} Bxc6 (15... Nxc6 16. Rxb7 Qxb7 17. Nd6+) 16. Rxb8+ ({An alternative way to win.} 16. Nxc6 Nxc6 17. Qa4 Qd7 18. Bxd5 exd5 19. Nb6 $18) 16... Rxb8 17.
Nxc6 Qc7 18. Nxb8 Qxc4 19. Bxd5 Qxd5 20. Qa4+ {Black resigned.} *

