1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Bd3 {This is a sideline. I would not normally recommend it as a permanent choice, but the circumstances were suitable here. Its advantage is that it can be learned quickly.} (3. exd5 {My former student had played this before. Like many beginners, he did not yet have a fixed weapon against every variation. For the Swiss Championship, however, this was not enough to obtain an advantage against a 1500-rated opponent.}) 3... c5 {This move had been prepared.} (
{We had also examined this.} 3... Nf6) (3... dxe4) 4. exd5 Qxd5 (4... exd5 {Although this creates a weak pawn, it would be clearly better than moving the queen repeatedly.}) 5.
Nc3 $1 {Understandably, my former student initially had serious reservations about sacrificing the pawn. The computer evaluation of about +1.5 convinced him, however. We had prepared the most important attacking ideas, especially the setup with queenside castling.} Qxd4 {Black accepts the sacrifice. Otherwise, Black would stand worse without compensation.} 6. Nb5 Qd7 7. Bf4 Na6 {Forced.} 8. Qe2 {With queenside castling. That was the whole idea.} Ne7 $4 (8... Nf6 9.
O-O-O {A great deal of active play for the pawn.}) 9. Nd6+ {Active moves.} Kd8 10. Nxf7+
Ke8 11. Nxh8 {With an extra rook, the position is won.} g6 12. O-O-O Nc6 13.
Bxg6+ {Tactics again.} Kd8 14. Nf7+ Ke8 15. Rxd7 Bxd7 16. Nd6+ Kd8 17. Bxh7
Nd4 18. Qe5 Nc7 19. Nxb7+ Ke8 20. Bg6+ Ke7 21. Bg5# *

