{The following example is a game between players rated approximately 1200 and 1450.} 
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d3 d6 6. O-O a6 7. a4 Ba7 8. Re1 h6
9. Nbd2 O-O 10. Nf1 Re8 {I had prepared my former student, who was playing Black, for this game. Up to this point, he follows our plan, while White also plays the opening very quickly.} 11. h3 Be6 12. Ba2 {From this point on, Black had to play independently. Please apply the middlegame rules from here.}
Bxa2 13. Rxa2 d5 $1 {[%csl Gd5,Ge5] Black has achieved the basic strategy of e5 openings.} 14. Qc2 d4 (14... Qd7 {[%cal Ga8d8] This would be my move in the position: improve the pieces. Black is not better, but it is easier for Black to find good moves.}) 15. Ng3 Qd7 16. Nf5 Rad8 {Black has activated all the pieces, while White has carried out the knight plan.} 17. Rd1 $2 dxc3 {This is something I wanted to demonstrate here. Black did not win automatically, but placed all the pieces actively, and such lines then emerge. Black could not have foreseen this, but only needs to find it once it is on the board.} 18. bxc3 Nxe4 {Black has won pawns. From now on, Black tries to exchange pieces by placing them actively.}
19. Ne3 Bxe3 20. Bxe3 Nf6 21. Raa1 Re6 {[%cal Ge6d6]} 22. Bc5 b6 23. Ba3 Qd5
24. Nd2 $2 {White loses another pawn.} Qxd3 25. Qb2 Qe2 26. Qc2 Ne4
$5 {Black finds a tactical way to exchange pieces.} (26... e4 {[%cal Ge4e3] White cannot escape the pin, however, so this would not be necessary.}) 27. Qxe4 Rxd2 28.
Qxe2 Rxe2 29. Rd7 Rc2 30. Rxc7 Rxc3 {We now have an endgame with two extra pawns. Technically, the position is won.} 31. Bc1 Nd4 $5 {Another exchange.} 32. Rxc3 Ne2+ 33. Kf1 Nxc3 34. Be3 Rd6 35. f3 Kf8 $2 {Black makes only one mistake and overlooks a tactic.} 36. a5 $1 $132 {Now there are problems.} Nd5 (36... b5 37. Bc5 {This is the point.}) 37. Bf2 Rc6 {Here Black allows another tactical possibility, although it is difficult to spot.} 38. axb6 ({A deeper tactic in the position would be} 38. Rd1 $1 {which even produces an advantage.} Nc3 39. axb6 $3 Nxd1 40. b7) 38... Nxb6 39. Rxa6
Rc1+ 40. Ke2 Nd5 $15 {Black is a pawn up, but objectively the position can be held because all the pawns are on one wing.} 41. Kd2 Nb4 $4 {Black pursues the sophisticated idea ...Rc2 and ...Nxa6, but overlooks an immediate tactic:} 42. Ra8+ Ke7 43. Bh4+ g5 44. Bxg5+ hxg5 45. Kxc1 {White now has a technically winning position and converted it.} Nd3+ 46. Kd2
Nc5 47. Ke3 Kf6 48. Rc8 Ne6 49. Ke4 {Black resigned.} *

