{The following online game shows how a correctly played game between strong players can develop. I played White.} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3.
d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nc6 5. Nc3 Nf6 6. Bg5 {Up to this point, it is theory: these are the most frequently played moves.} h6 $6 (6... e6 {Is the "correct" continuation, in case you are wondering where Black's "mistake" occurred.}) 7. Bxf6 gxf6 {Now our rules: first check active moves. There are none. Then apply the opening rules, which tell us to develop. The bishop is best placed on b5.} 8. Bb5 (8. Bc4
e6 {Bc4 points at nothing here.}) 8... Bd7 9. O-O {White has followed the opening rules.} Qb6 {Active moves come first, and Nd5 is one of them.} 10. Nd5 $5 (10.
Nf5 {The computer correctly says, for example, that this move is even stronger, but finding it requires positional understanding. Nd5 is only the second-best move, but it is easier to see.}) 10... Qxd4 11. Bxc6 {An intermediate move.} (11. Nc7+ Kd8 12.
Nxa8 Qxd1 13. Rfxd1 Kc8 {Na8 is trapped.}) 11... Qxd1 (11... Bxc6 12. Qxd4)
12. Bxd7+ Kxd7 13. Rfxd1 {White has not won material through this sequence, but the game has been simplified into an endgame. Black has lost the bishop pair and remains saddled with weak pawns.} Bg7 14. c3 {Next, White secures the position. This is called consolidation.} e6 15. Ne3
Rac8 16. Rd3 Ke7 17. Rad1 Rhd8 $16 {[%cal Gg1f1,Gc3c4,Gb2b3,Gf1e2] White now has a long-term static advantage. Play proceeds very slowly, and White must demonstrate technique. Correct play will be covered in detail from Part 20 onwards, but useful rules here would include activating the king, placing pawns on light squares because Black has a dark-squared bishop, exchanging rooks if possible, and so on.} *

