{The following game was played between two players rated approximately 1300. Black was played by my former student.} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5.
d4 exd4 6. e5 {Up to this point, White plays the opening very quickly; the variation was probably prepared.} d5 $1 {An essential move; otherwise Black is losing.} 7. Bb5 Ne4 8. cxd4 Bb4+ (8... Bb6 {Theory says this is more precise, but that belongs more to opening theory. Objectively, ...Bb6 is not better, but in practice ...Bb6 is easier and more sensible.} 9. Be3 (9. Bxc6+ bxc6 {The doubled pawn is not a problem because of ...c5, and ...Ba6 is also threatened.}) 9... O-O 10. Nc3 Bg4 $132) 9. Bd2 Nxd2 10. Nbxd2 {The real game begins only now, because this is the first point at which both players must think independently.} (10. Bxc6+ {If White insists on giving Black doubled pawns, Black can save the position.} bxc6 11. Nbxd2 c5 $132 (11... Ba6 {[%cal Ra6f1] This is also an argument, although White's active moves must be considered here.} 12.
Qa4 Bxd2+ 13. Nxd2 Bb5 $13 {[%cal Gb8b1] In return for the doubled pawn, White must play with an unsafe king.})) 10... Bg4 $6 {[%cal Rb5c6] This is still the opening, but I would already think in middlegame terms. ...Bg4 is an active move, but on the one hand it does not fit the position strategically. More importantly, when checking active moves...} (10... Bd7 {Avoids doubled pawns and is the simplest solution. There is even a discovered attack threatened.} 11. O-O $2 Nxe5 $1) (10... O-O {Preserves flexibility. For example, we can now play ...Ne7 or, after an exchange, ...Ba6.} 11.
O-O (11. Bxc6 bxc6 12. O-O (12. Rc1 Ba6 $5 {Now White cannot castle at all.}
) 12... c5 $132) 11... Ne7 $5 {The knight is badly placed on c6 anyway.} 12. a3
Ba5 13. b4 Bb6 14. Rc1 c6 15. Bd3 Bg4 $11) 11. Bxc6+ bxc6 {Now c6 is extremely weak...} 12. Qa4 (12. Rc1 {This is no less a reason to call 10...Bg4 a mistake. Although correct play does not destroy Black's position, it does create certain problems.} Qd7 13. Qc2 {The c6-pawn is lost. The only point is that Black can sacrifice the pawn.} Bxf3 14. gxf3 O-O 15. Qxc6 $140
Qh3 $13 16. Qxd5 (16. Qxc7 $4 Rac8) 16... Rad8 $44 {This variation is clear to me as an IM, but for a beginner, Rc1 should trigger a red warning light marked "problems".}) 12... Bxd2+ 13. Nxd2 Bd7 14. Qa3 {Black now has a bad bishop and weak pawns.} Qg5 $1 {[%cal Rg5g2] Black seeks counterplay. In general, that is good.} (14... Qe7 15. Qxe7+ Kxe7 16. Nb3 $16 {Would lead to a very bad endgame for Black. This is what White wants.}) 15.
Nf3 (15. O-O $2 Qxd2) (15. Qg3 $1 {White should instead insist on exchanging queens and allow no counterplay.} Qh6 16. Nb3 O-O 17. O-O {Positionally, Black's position is a disaster.}) 15... Qg6 $4 (15... Qxg2 {Of course this does not win a pawn, but Black has to do something. In a slow game, Black loses without a fight.} 16. Rg1 Qh3 17. Rxg7 Bg4 {If Black creates problems for White, Black might even win outright...} 18. Nd2 ({And now it depends on whether White finds the move. From Black's perspective, it is nevertheless the best practical chance. You cannot predict whether such tactical blows will exist.} 18. Rxf7 $3 Kxf7 19. Ng5+ $18) 18... Qxa3
19. bxa3 $132 {This is much easier to play than the game continuation.}) 16. O-O
Bg4 17. Nd2 {Black has no counterplay and gradually loses the game.} Qe6 18. Rac1 Be2 19. Rfe1 Bb5 20. Qa5 Qd7 21. a4 Bd3 22. Re3 Bg6 23. Rec3 Rb8
24. Rxc6 O-O 25. Rxc7 Qg4 26. Qxd5 Rxb2 {At last the counterplay arrives, but far too late.} 27. h3 Qe2 28. Rc8 $1 {A good move by White.} Rb8 (28... Rxd2
29. Rxf8+ Kxf8 30. Qd8#) 29. Rxf8+ Rxf8 30. Nb3 Be4 31. Qd7 Qb2 32. Rc8 {More exchanges.} Qa3 33. Rxf8+ Qxf8 34. Nc5 {Activate the pieces. Prevents Qf8 from emerging.} Bc2 35. Qxa7 h6 36. a5 Qd8 37. Nd7 Ba4 38. Qb8 {The final exchange.}
Qxb8 39. Nxb8 {Without queens, the position is hopeless.} Kf8 40. a6 Bb3 41. a7 Bd5 42.
Nd7+ Ke7 43. Nb6 Bb7 44. a8=Q Bxa8 45. Nxa8 Kd7 *

