{My former student, rated approximately 1700, played the following game against a player rated around 1500.} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. g3 {I had recommended this move to my former student for the game in order to surprise Black. Black responds well, however, and the move eventually becomes a problem for White.} Bc5 (4... d5 $1
{Is the other good continuation.} 5. exd5 Nxd5 6. Bg2 Nxc3 7. bxc3 {Both sides have a centre.}) 5. Bg2 d6 6. O-O O-O 7.
d3 {Black now has the active Bc5 against the very poor Bg2. White's idea, however, is to prepare f4 and adopt a kingside setup. That was the reason for playing Bg2.} Bg4 (7... a6 {If you know the theory—which I was certain Black would not know in this variation—you should play ...a6 to protect Bc5 against Na4.} 8. h3 Be6 9. Kh2 h6 10. Nh4 {This is roughly how the game continues. White's entire hope is that Black does not play ...d5; then White gains a strategic advantage after f4.} d5 $1 {But if Black knows and plays ...d5, White's idea with Bg2 has no value at all.}) 8. h3 Be6 9. Na4 Bb6 10. Nxb6 axb6 {White is now indeed better because the bishop is slightly stronger than the knight. To exploit this, however, White should choose the correct strategy: playing d4.} 11. Ng5 {White insists on f4 as the plan, but the knight is then no worse than the bishop, and play on the wing is also inferior to play in the centre.} (11. Re1 $1 Re8 (11... d5 {Does not work.} 12. exd5 Nxd5 13.
Nxe5) 12. d4 $1 exd4 13. Nxd4 Nxd4 14. Qxd4 {White has achieved d4 and has a small advantage because of the bishop pair.} Rxa2 $2 15. Rxa2 Bxa2 16. b3 {[%csl Ra2] The bishop is trapped.}) 11... Qd7 12. f4 Qe7 {Black has simply lost time.} 13. f5 {White persists with the plan from move four. But bishops are not good in closed positions.} (13. Nxe6 {This is what I would have played here.} Qxe6 14. a3 {And now open the position and make use of the bishop pair.}) 13... Bd7 14. c4 $6 {This move is very poor, although I have to praise White. White saw that Black could play ...d5 and tried to prevent it prophylactically. The understanding is there, but c4 makes the position worse overall.} (14. g4 {Continuing with an attack would be better. At least ...d5 is not an immediate threat.}) 14... Nd4 {A weak square and an outpost for the knight.} 15. Be3 c5 $6 16. g4 (16. a4 $1 $16 {If we are already thinking prophylactically, this would be a grandmasterly move. Black can no longer break free because the only move, ...b5, has been taken out of the position. White can now play on the kingside without much thought.}) 16... h6 17. Bxd4 $2 {White now exchanges the good bishop. It is stronger than the Ng5 that White wanted to save.} cxd4 18. Nf3 b5 {Now Black's counterplay begins. It is much faster than White's play on the kingside.} 19. b3 $6 Bc6 $6 20.
Nd2 $6 Nd7 21. Qf3 Qg5 {Now the end begins: instead of an attack, White merely has holes on the kingside. Bg2 has been bad since move four.} 22. cxb5 Qxd2 23. bxc6 bxc6 {This is now a model position illustrating a bad bishop. I will only add that White managed to hold the game with some luck, but from this position onward it was a one-sided endgame for Black. The remainder of the game follows without further comments.} 24. Rfd1 Qb4 25. h4 f6 26. Qe2 Nc5 27. Rdc1 Qb5 28. Rd1 Ra5 29. Qd2 Na6 30. a4 Qb4 31. Qxb4 Nxb4 32. Rac1 Rc8 33. Rc4 Na2 34. Ra1 Nc3 35. b4 Rxa4 36. Rxa4 Nxa4 37. b5 Nb6 38. Rxc6 Rxc6 39. bxc6 Kf7 40. Bf3 Ke7 41. Bd1 Kd8 42. Bb3 Kc7 43. Bd5 Nc8 44. Be6 Na7 45. Kg2 Nxc6 46. Kg3 Nb4 47. Bc4 Kc6 48. g5 h5 49. gxf6 gxf6 50. Kf3 Kc7 51. Ke2 Kd7 52. Bf7 Ke7 53. Bxh5 d5 54. Bf3 dxe4 55. dxe4 Kf8 56. Kd2 Kg7 57. Ke2 Kh6 58. Kd2 Kg7 59. Be2 Kh6 60. Kc1 Kg7 61. Kb2 Nc6 62. Kb3 Ne7 63. Kc4 Kf7 64. Kc5 Nc8 65. Kc6 Ne7+ 66. Kd6 Ng8 67. Kd7 Kf8 68. Ke6 Kg7 69. h5 Nh6 70. Ke7 Ng8+ 71. Ke8 Nh6 72. Bf3 Ng8 *

