{The game comes from the German U10 Championship. It shows how simple ideas can put even a future runner-up under pressure.} 1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. Bd3 Nf6 5. h3 {This move does not follow the opening rules, but it is strong. Its idea is to deprive Bc8 of squares.} (5. c3 Bg4 {Otherwise it develops via g4 before Black plays ...e6.}) 5... Nc6 6. c3 e5 $5 {This was still part of Black's preparation. Black even knew this sideline. Black accepts an isolated d5-pawn in return for ensuring that Bc8 will not be bad.} 7. dxe5
Nxe5 8. Nf3 Nxd3+ 9. Qxd3 Bd6 {Both sides played quickly up to this point, and only now does the real battle begin. We examine it using our rules.} 10. Bg5 {Develops the bishop and indirectly attacks d5. A good move.} O-O 11. O-O (11. Bxf6 Qxf6 12.
Qxd5 Re8+ {Wins the pawn, but it is not worth taking.}) 11... Bc7 $5 {Black offers the d5-pawn. Should we take it?} 12. Nbd2 (
12. Bxf6 Qxf6 13. Qxd5 Rd8 $44 {Could be unpleasant; among other things, ...Bxh3 is threatened.}) 12... h6 13. Bxf6 Qxf6 {Second opportunity: should White take the pawn now?} 14. Qxd5 (14. Nd4 {Although this is not a bad move, White is not forced to take the pawn. The knights are well placed and Black threatens nothing. The pawn keeps the position more closed and denies Black open files and diagonals for the pieces.} Re8 15. Rfe1 Bd7 16. N2f3 (16. Nf1 $5 {[%cal Gf1e3]})) 14...
Rd8 15. Qe4 Be6 {Black has sacrificed the pawn and is playing actively. What should White do? Exchange pieces, yes—but without allowing Black to dictate the exchanges.} 16. Nd4 (16. Qxb7 {Is possible, but White decided against it. From a practical perspective, that is good: one extra pawn is enough, so the first priority is not to lose.}) (16.
Rfe1 {Is also good: it activates a piece and, in addition...} Bd5 17. Qe7
) 16... Bd5 17. Qg4 Qd6 18.
N2f3 {So far everything is going very well for White: the pieces are active and the extra pawn has been retained.} a6 {Black prevents Nb5 by exchanging.} 19.
Nf5 $2 {The threat is strong, but the move is good only if White wins something with it. Calculation shows that this is not the case. Therefore, the move must be evaluated positionally, and Nf5 is less secure and worse placed on f5 than on d4. In other words, Nf5 should be played only if it fits the plan. It does not.} ({Instead, if White has no plan, the emergency option is to activate the pieces. Now Nf5 really is a threat because it exchanges pieces.} 19. Rfe1 $14 {[%cal Yd4f5,Yf5e7]}) 19... Qf6 20. Rfe1 $2 {Rule violation number two: you must consider your opponent's active moves.} (20. N5d4 {Loses a tempo, but the static advantage—the extra pawn—remains. This would be the best decision. An important point in chess: you must evaluate the resulting position, not the moves themselves. Although Nf5 was a mistake, White is still doing well.})
20... Be6 21. N3d4 {White thought for twenty minutes here, so Be6 was clearly overlooked rather than merely misjudged. White now has no other choice.} Rxd4 $1 22.
Nxh6+ {At least White takes another pawn along the way.} (22. Rxe6
$5 {This also has to be calculated. I cannot say whether White rejected the move or never considered it. Correctly calculating the variation in a classical game would, however, require a playing strength of at least 2000 Elo, in my estimation.} Rxg4 (22... Qxe6 23. Qxg7#) (22... fxe6 23. Nxd4) 23. Rxf6
Re4 $1 {Strangely, White now has no squares for the rook. Spotting this "quiet" move and realising that Rf6 is trapped is very difficult. White is therefore forced to...} (23... Rf4 $2 24. Ne7+ Kf8 25. Rxf4 Bxf4 26. Nd5
{An extra pawn in the endgame.}) 24. Nxh6+ (24. Nd6 Re2 25. Rxf7 (25. Nxf7 gxf6) 25...
Bxd6) 24... gxh6 25. Rxh6 {Technically, White has three pawns for the bishop, so materially the position looks acceptable. The problem is that Black's rooks are more active in this endgame, giving Black a large advantage. Correctly assessing this—especially as a blindfold position, where moves cannot be checked quickly—is something that comes only with playing strength.} Re2 26. b3
Rd8 {This is only an example; it is more about evaluating the position than the concrete variation.} 27. a4 Rd3 28. Rc1 Bf4) 22... Qxh6
23. Qxd4 Rd8 24. Qe4 {Materially, the position would be almost acceptable if White could reach an endgame. Black deserves credit, however, for attacking instead of defending pawns.} Qg5 $1 {The exclamation mark does not mean that this is the best move, but that Black is following the correct strategy.} (24... b5 25.
Rad1 {Gives White good saving chances. In a pure endgame, White could even play for a win.} Rxd1 26. Rxd1 Bxa2 {Black has won a pawn, but White has counterplay. For example:} 27. Qe7 $132 Bb6 28. Qd6 {And White exchanges queens.} a5
29. Qxh6 gxh6 30. Rd7 $11 {White will successfully exchange pawns on the queenside.}) 25. Kh1 (25. Rad1 $5 {This is another point that only a strong player can assess correctly. The attack is so dangerous that White should prefer to sacrifice a pawn and reach an endgame rather than continue defending.} Bxh3 $2 {Shows why Rad1 is better.} 26. Rxd8+ Bxd8 27. Qxb7 {Is excellent for White.}) (25. Qxb7 $4 Bd5 26. Qxc7 Qxg2#) 25... Bf5 26. Qxb7 {Black had naturally calculated this.} Qf4 27. g3 Qxf2 28. Rg1 (28. Qxc7 Rd2 {With checkmate.}) 28... Bxg3 (28... Rd2 29.
Rg2 {Was the idea.}) 29. Rg2 Qe3 30. Rf1 Qe4 {Black does not have to, but can simply win in the endgame.} 31. Qb3 (31. Qxe4 Bxe4 {An extra piece.}) 31...
Rd2 32. Rfg1 Bxh3 *

