1. e4 d5 {I had prepared my former student, who was playing White, for this game. We knew that Black played this opening, and I knew a grandmaster-level refutation of the variation. I had shown these lines to my student.} 2. exd5 Nf6 3. d4 Nxd5 4. c4 Nb6 5. Nf3 Bf5 $16 {I had not shown this move because it is objectively weaker. White now had to play independently; nevertheless, the aim of the preparation—an objective advantage for White—had been achieved.} ({The main moves here are} 5... g6 6. Nc3 Bg7 7. Be3 O-O 8. h3 Nc6 9. Qd2 {With queenside castling at this point.}) ({And} 5... Bg4 {But you can look up the refutation yourselves, or watch from Part 40 onwards.}) 6. Nc3 {This move follows the rules.} e6 7. Be3 {This move was correct in the ...g6 variation, but there we castled queenside.} (7.
Bd3 {This would be a normal opening move.} Bxd3 8. Qxd3 Be7 9. O-O O-O 10. Bf4
Nc6 11. Rad1 Re8 12. Rfe1 {Would be an example of following my rules. The win is still a long way off, but White is unquestionably better.}) 7... Be7 8. h3 $6 {Instead of thinking independently or following the rules, White blindly plays the ...g6 variation from move five.} h6 {[%cal Gg2g4,Gf1g2,Yf1e2,Ye1g1,Rf1d3,Gd1b3,Ye1c1] If White thought independently, there would be many ideas: Qb3 with 0-0-0, Bd3 with 0-0, or Be2 with 0-0. Each of these moves follows the opening rules. g4 does not.} 9. g4 $6 {The computer would not regard g4 as a mistake, but I do. There is a deep strategic reason why it can be good with correct play. White did not have that reason in mind, however, and broke established rules to play it.} ({The plan of attacking on the kingside is even possible, and White could have followed it.} 9. Qd2 O-O 10. O-O-O Nc6 11. g4
Bh7 12. h4 $40) 9... Bh7 10. b3 $2 {Again, a move that does not follow the rules.} Nc6 {Black does not play the following phase perfectly either, but I want to comment only on White's decisions.} 11. Qd2 {What happened to castling? White does not intend to castle queenside here at all.} Ba3 {Is unnecessary.} (11... O-O 12. O-O-O $4 Ba3+
{b3 has ruined everything.}) 12. Bg2 Nb4
13. O-O Nc2 14. Rad1 Nxe3 15. Qxe3 (15. fxe3 $5 {[%cal Ge3d4,Yf1f8] I prefer this because it strengthens the centre and activates Rf1.}) 15... O-O 16. d5 $2 {This is a decisive strategic mistake. All of the following is advanced, but it would not have happened if my rules had been followed. Expert assessment: White has more space and more active pieces, while Black's minor pieces do nothing, so restricting Black would be the correct plan. Beginner's version: do you have a plan for White? If not, activate the pieces. That covers the positional aspect. There is also a tactical point:} (16. Rfe1 {And then continuing to think would follow the basic rules.}) (16. Ne5 c6 17. d5 {Even this would be a better version of the game.} cxd5 18. cxd5 exd5 19. Nxd5 Nxd5 20. Rxd5 {The bishop on g2 is free here.}) (
16. c5 $1 {This is a variation that only a titled player might find—and even then not certainly.} Nd5 (16... Nd7 17. Nb5 Bb4 18. a3 Ba5 19. b4 c6 {Black can still create some complications and avoid being a full piece down, but White remains much better.} 20. bxa5 (20. Nd6 Bc7 21. Nxb7 Qb8
22. Na5 Bxa5 23. bxa5 Qb5 24. Ne5 Nxe5 25. dxe5 {Then Rd6.}) 20... cxb5 21. Ne5
Nxe5 22. dxe5 Qxa5 23. Bxb7 Rab8 24. c6) 17. Nxd5 exd5 18. b4 $3 {Considering this move and examining it further requires very good technique or positional understanding.} Bxb4 19. Qb3 Ba5 (19... a5 20. a3) 20. Qxb7 {White has a large positional advantage because Ba5 has hardly any squares. For a grandmaster, White's position is winning.}) 16... exd5 17. Nxd5 Nxd5 18. Rxd5 Qf6 {White has now exchanged the poorly placed Nb6.} 19. g5 $2 {Follows neither a plan nor the emergency option. Instead, it even weakens White's own pawn structure.} (19. Re1 {Black cannot do the same.} Rad8 20. Rxd8 Rxd8 21. Qxa7) (19. Rfd1 Rfe8 20. Qd4 {White has active pieces.}) 19... hxg5 20. Qxg5 Qxg5
21. Nxg5 Rad8 {I am still commenting only on White's moves here.} 22. Rxd8 (22. Ra5 $1 {White has won the pawn almost automatically, but it would still be good to notice the move and compare the options. Here White wins two pawns.} Bb4 (22... Bd6 23. Bxb7
) 23. Rxa7 b6 24. Rxc7) 22... Rxd8 23. Nxh7
Kxh7 24. Bxb7 Rd2 {White can hardly defend the pawn, but found a way.} 25. Be4+ g6 26. Bb1 {This was unwise, however, because Bb1 is badly placed, a2 is difficult to hold, and Black could dominate. I cannot blame White for any of this, because these are advanced concepts. I leave the rest of the game uncommented.} Kh6 27. Kg2 Kg5 28. Kg3 Bd6+ 29. f4+ Kh5 30. Re1 g5 31. Kf3 Bxf4 32. Re8 Rd6 33. Rh8+ Rh6 34. Rxh6+ Kxh6 {Opposite-coloured bishop endings are drawn, but White misjudged the position here and therefore lost. White tried to play for a win instead of accepting the draw.} 35. Ke4 (35. Kg4) (35. h4 {Would be safe ways to draw.}) 35... Kh5 36. Kd5 Kh4 37. Bf5 Be3 38. Kc6
Kg3 39. Kxc7 Kf4 40. Bg4 $4 (40. Bc8) 40... f5 41. Bxf5 (41. Bd1 Kg3) 41...
Kxf5 42. b4 Kf4 43. c5 Kg3 44. a4 $6 (44. c6 Kxh3 45. Kb7 Bf4 46. Kxa7 (46. c7
Bxc7 47. Kxc7 g4 48. Kb7 g3 49. Kxa7 g2 50. b5 g1=Q+ 51. b6 Qc5) 46... g4 47.
b5 g3 48. b6 g2 49. c7 Bxc7 50. bxc7 g1=Q+) 44... Kxh3 45. c6 Bf4+ 46. Kd7 g4
47. b5 g3 48. a5 g2 49. b6 g1=Q 50. c7 Qd4+ 51. Kc6 Qe4+ 52. Kd7 Qd5+ 53. Kc8
Qxa5 *

