1. e4 c6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 Bg4 4. h3 Bxf3 5. Qxf3 e6 6. d3 (6. Be2 Nf6 7. O-O {This is what the main line looks like, for example.}) 6... Nd7 7. Bd2 {With d3 and Bd2, White has chosen a sideline and intends to castle queenside.} Bc5 8.
g4 $5 {Only now does Black have to think for the first time.} Qh4 {A good move that prevents queenside castling.} 9. Ne2 {Makes sense insofar as White can play d3-d4. How should Black continue now?} (9. O-O-O Qxf2 $17) 9... dxe4 (
9... Ne5 {Suddenly there is a tactic that both players overlooked.}
10. Qf4 (10. Qg3 Qxg3 11. fxg3 (11. Nxg3 Nf3+ 12. Ke2 Nxd2 13. Kxd2 Bxf2) 11...
Nf3+ 12. Kd1 dxe4 {Also wins a pawn.} 13. dxe4 $2 O-O-O {A piece has been won.}) 10... Bxf2+ {This is the actual tactical blow. Black has won pawns.} 11. Qxf2 $140 (11. Kd1 Ng6 12. Qc7 Qe7) 11... Nf3+) 10. dxe4 Qf6 (10... Ne5 {Is still possible, but Black does not check any active moves.}) 11. Qxf6 Ngxf6 {Here Black has no advantage.} 12. Nc3 Ne5 13. Be2 O-O-O 14. f4 Ng6 {Although Black later won the game, White is actually slightly better here.} *

