1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 exd4 4. Bc4 Bb4+ $5 {If you are prepared to learn a great deal of theory—or happen to have the right analysis before the game—you can play a very precise response to a gambit.} (4... Nf6 {An interesting side note: Black normally plays this variation instead.} 5. e5 d5 $1 6. Bb5 Ne4 {It is also easier to remember, especially because this gambit is rarely played above beginner level. Since we knew that this variation might appear on the board, however, I prepared Black for it using my own analysis.}) (4... Bc5 5. O-O Nf6 6. e5 d5 7. exf6 dxc4 8. fxg7 {This is the other common option, for which White was also prepared.}) 5. c3 dxc3 6. O-O Nf6 7.
Nxc3 Bxc3 8. bxc3 d6 {This was exactly the preparation, and Black is simply a pawn up. With perfect play, the computer still finds just enough compensation for White. After almost all natural continuations, however, Black keeps the extra pawn. Side note: this is why you should not play supposedly simple gambits if you want to progress far in chess.} 9.
Re1 O-O 10. Bf4 Bg4 $1 {Black follows one of my rules here, which was familiar from the preparation: when you are a pawn up, exchange pieces.} 11. Qd3 (11. h3 Bxf3 12. Qxf3 Ne5 {Is the same.}) 11... Bxf3 $1 {Simplify into an endgame with an extra pawn—perfect. The move is good only because ...Ne5 is possible afterwards, however. Otherwise, the good bishop should not be exchanged for the knight without good reason.} 12. Qxf3 Ne5 13. Bxe5 dxe5
14. Rab1 b6 {Black still has the extra pawn, and White has hardly any counterplay.} 15. Qf5 Qe7 16. Rbd1 Rad8 {Black has played everything perfectly so far. Not even the computer could have played it better.} 17. Bd5 Nxd5 $6 {This move is a mistake after all, but a mistake at a high level. The advanced explanation: Bd5 is a bad, useless bishop. Black therefore does not need to exchange it for the considerably stronger knight (Value of the Pieces → 6.2). It would be more precise simply to improve Black's position. White can do very little with this bad bishop. Black feared f4 here and wanted to simplify the position with ...Nxd5, avoiding those complications. The concern was understandable, but the concrete assessment of f4 was incorrect.} (17... Rd6 18. h3 {To show what the position looks like without exchanging the bishop and why it is better.} (18. f4 Re8
{Is no problem at all.} (18... exf4 {Must of course be checked.} 19. e5 Rxd5
{Now e5 is pinned?} 20. exf6 Rxf5 21. fxe7 Re8 22. Rd8 {The variation ends in White's favour, so capturing is not the simple solution.})) 18...
Rfd8 19. Bb3 Rxd1 20. Rxd1 Rxd1+ 21. Bxd1 Qd7 22. Qxd7 Nxd7 {This is the advanced rule about active pieces from Part 10 onwards. If your opponent has a bad bishop, exchange everything except that bad bishop and win the endgame. That is the case here.} 23. f4 f6 24. Kf2 Kf7 25. Ke3
Ke7 {This is a very simple win for Black; it merely takes more moves.}) ({Other moves that improve the position include} 17... Rfe8 18. Rd3 Qc5 19. Rg3 Kf8
{[%cal Rc7c6,Rd8d2]}) (17... Qc5 {[%cal Rc5c3] Also prevents f4.} 18. Qxe5
$4 (18. Rd3 $4 c6) 18... c6) 18. exd5 Rfe8 {The fact that Black played inaccurately does not mean that White is close to equality. Black still has a large advantage.} 19. Qe4 Qc5 20. Rd3 c6 $5 {Black opens files for the rooks. The only minor drawback is that it exchanges pawns for the later endgame.} (20... Rd6 {A siege with ...Rd6 would be the quieter plan. Black can still play ...c6 later.}) 21. Rh3 $2 {Counterplay is generally good, but here it merely loses time.} (21.
dxc6 Rxd3 22. Qxd3 Qxc6 $17 {See the game continuation.}) 21... h6
22. dxc6 Rd6 23. Rd3 Rxd3 24. Qxd3 Qxc6 {Black is a pawn up for the endgame and only has to ensure that White does not exchange too many pawns.} 25. f3 f6 {Safely defends e5; a good move.} 26. Kf2 Rc8 27. Re3 Qc4 {Offers a queen exchange.} 28. Qc2 {[%cal Gc8d8,Rb6b5,Rb5b4] Something paradoxical now happened: Black feared perpetual check and therefore agreed to a repetition. Black should absolutely continue here and simply improve the position. Consider it from the other side as well: Black is under no pressure to win immediately. White, by contrast, must demonstrate how the position can be held.} Qc5 (28... b5 29. a3 a5 30. Qg6 Rd8 {I see no threats for White.}) 29. Qb3+ Qc4 30.
Qc2 Qc5 31. Qb3+ *

