1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d4 exd4 6. cxd4 Bb4+ 7. Nbd2 $6 (7.
Bd2 Bxd2+ 8. Nbxd2 d5 $1 9. exd5 Nxd5 10. O-O O-O {Was the general preparation against this typical gambit. White has an isolated pawn, which makes Black's position more pleasant to play.}) 7... d5 $1 8. exd5 Nxd5 9. O-O O-O {The middlegame now begins.} 10. a3 Be7 {It is good not to exchange. The bishops are slightly better in this position, and Nd2 is not dangerous.} 11. h3 $2 {Does nothing useful for White. In addition,} Nb6 $1 {[%cal Rc6d4] White now loses the weak pawn.} 12. Qb3 (12. Nb3 {Defends the pawn but loses the bishop.}
Nxc4) 12... Nxd4 13. Nxd4 Qxd4 14. Re1 Bc5 15. Qe3 Qxe3 16. fxe3 {From this point on, the task is to convert the advantage. Black is happy to enter the endgame.} Re8 {More precise than taking on c4 immediately.} 17. Kf2 Nxc4 18. Nxc4 b6 {Black has the bishop pair, an extra pawn, and the better pawn structure.} 19. b4 Be7 20. Bb2 Ba6 21. Rac1 Rad8 (
21... c5 $1 {I would very much like this move here. It does exchange a pawn, but c7 is weak, and afterwards White has no targets to attack.}) 22. Be5 Bxc4
$5 {Black finds a way to exchange more pieces.} (22... c5 {Would still be good.}) 23. Rxc4 Bh4+ $1 24. Bg3 (24. Rxh4 Rxe5) 24... Bxg3+ 25. Kxg3 Rc8 26. Kf3 (
26. Rec1 Rxe3+) 26... c5 27. Rec1 Re5 {It is technically difficult to win, but at least Black is clearly the only side playing for a win.} 28. R1c3 f6 29.
bxc5 Rcxc5 30. Rxc5 bxc5 31. Rb3 c4 32. Rb8+ Kf7 33. Rc8 Ra5 34. Rxc4 Rxa3 {I do not want to comment on the remainder of the game and instead refer to Part 20, where I will discuss this in detail. Only one point on move 46 is interesting.} 35. Rd4 Ra1 36. Rd7+ Kg6 37. Kg4 a5 38. Ra7 a4 39. h4 h5+
40. Kf4 a3 41. Ke4 a2 42. Ra6 Rh1 43. Rxa2 Rxh4+ 44. Kf3 Rh1 45. Rf2 h4 46. Rd2
h3 {A draw if the pawns are exchanged.} 47. g4 $2 (47. gxh3 {Is objectively drawn here; White must exchange pawns. In a rook ending, one extra pawn is often not enough.}) 47... h2 48. Kg2 Rg1+ 49. Kxh2 Rxg4 {The pawn was exchanged here as well, but because of the small detail that the king is cut off, the position is lost.} 50. Rg2 $4 {But the exchange into a pawn ending must be avoided; that position is hopeless.} Rxg2+ 51. Kxg2 Kf5 52. Kf3 Ke5 53. e4 f5
54. exf5 Kxf5 55. Kg3 Kg5 56. Kf2 Kf4 57. Kg2 Kg4 58. Kh1 Kg3 59. Kg1 g6 *
